@database "comfyui"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: comfyui.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "ComfyUI"
@{h1}ComfyUI

An agent that should make an image, a sound, a video or a text with a
ComfyUI server uses this plugin. You export a workflow from ComfyUI and
name the inputs an agent may set -- the prompt, the seed, the size; the
agent picks a workflow, fills in those inputs and gets the files back,
and can look at them itself. With several ComfyUI servers the plugin hands
each job to the least busy one. Every job is recorded; the @{b}ComfyUI@{ub}
panel shows the servers and the jobs and lets you cancel one.

@{image docs/panel.png "The ComfyUI panel with two servers and their jobs"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The ComfyUI panel  " link panel}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The workflow tool  " link tool}
  @{"  Waiting, results and files  " link results}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Workflows  " link workflows}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The ComfyUI panel"
@{h1}The ComfyUI panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Agents & tools@{ub}, "ComfyUI"). It shows
the jobs of every session and every agent; it does not follow the chat.

@{image docs/panel.png "The ComfyUI panel"}

The panel loads every 5 seconds from the moment it opens; the menu next to
the reload button sets another interval or turns it off, and the toolbar
shows when it last loaded.

@{h2}Before it lists anything

Each load first asks the servers what became of the jobs still open, and
records it:

@{bullet}a job a server runs is marked running;
@{bullet}a job no reachable server has in its queue is looked up in the
history of the server that got it: completed or failed as it says there,
and failed as @{i}Job lost (server restart or queue cleared)@{ui} when it is
not there either;
@{bullet}a job on a server that cannot be reached is left as it was; with
no server reachable, nothing is changed.

@{h2}What you see

@{bullet}@{b}Servers@{ub}: every configured server, online with how many
jobs it runs and has queued, or offline with the reason.
@{bullet}Five figures: jobs active (queued or running), completed, failed,
cancelled, and all recorded.
@{bullet}@{b}Active jobs@{ub}, oldest first: the start of the job id (the
whole id on hover), the workflow, the status, when it was submitted and
how long it has run -- or waited, while it has not started.
@{bullet}@{b}Recently finished@{ub}: the last ten, newest first, with the
outcome, the error of a failed one, when and after how long it finished,
and how many images, sounds, videos and texts it made.

A click on a column head sorts by that column; the order holds when the
list reloads.

@{h2}Cancelling a job

@{b}Cancel@{ub} on an active job asks first. Confirmed, a queued job is
taken out of its server's queue, a running one is interrupted on the
server that runs it. The list loads anew either way. When the server
refuses or cannot be reached, or the job finished in the meantime, the
panel says so and the job keeps what it had or gets its real outcome.

@{h2}When the list cannot be loaded

The panel says @{i}Jobs could not be loaded@{ui} with the reason and shows
nothing of before, so nothing out of date passes for current.
@endnode

@node tool "The workflow tool"
@{h1}The workflow tool

The plugin gives an agent one tool, @{tt}comfyui_workflow@{utt} (the
plugin instance's name followed by @{tt}_workflow@{utt}). Every call names
an @{tt}operation@{utt}.

@{h2}Operations

@{table}
operation | Needs | Does
list | -- | the configured workflows with their parameters; category filters
execute | workflow_id | queues the workflow with the given parameters; answers the prompt_id
wait_for_completion | prompt_id | waits until the job is done, failed, lost or timed out
status | prompt_id | the job's state now
result | prompt_id | the job's outputs, downloaded
load | prompt_id, file_path or filename | attaches files made before, for the model to look at
cancel | prompt_id | takes a queued job out of the queue, interrupts a running one
server_status | -- | whether the primary server answers, and its queue lengths
queue | -- | the primary server's queue
upload_image | file_path | copies a local image into ComfyUI's input folder
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
workflow_id | execute | an id that list returns
parameters | execute | the workflow's inputs as a JSON object in a string, e.g. {"prompt": "a lighthouse at dusk", "seed": 7}
category | list | only the workflows of that category
prompt_id | status, result, wait_for_completion, cancel, load | the id execute returned
poll_interval | wait_for_completion | seconds between looks, at least 1; without it 2
include_content | result, wait_for_completion | true: the outputs are attached for the model; default false
download | result | false: nothing is saved, only listed; default true
output_prefix | result, wait_for_completion | saved files are named <prefix>_<name>; 1 to 64 letters, digits, _ . -; default comfy
file_path | load, upload_image | load: a file in the output folder; upload_image: the image to upload
filename | load | a file name looked for in the output folder
@{body}

A parameter the workflow does not name is ignored. One it names but the
call leaves out gets its default from the workflow's configuration; with
no default the workflow file keeps its own value. A parameter marked
required and left out fails the call.

@{h2}execute

The job goes to the least busy server (see @{" Setting it up " link setup})
and is recorded with that server; every later call about it asks that
server. The answer: @{tt}status: queued@{utt}, @{tt}prompt_id@{utt},
@{tt}workflow_id@{utt}, @{tt}workflow_name@{utt}.

An image input -- a parameter that sets @{tt}inputs.image@{utt} of a
@{tt}LoadImage@{utt} node -- may name a local file: a path, or just the
name of a file the plugin downloaded before (the newest of that name
wins). The plugin uploads it to the server that runs the job and puts the
server's name for it into the workflow. A value that is no such file is
passed on as it is, as the name of a file already in ComfyUI's input
folder. Only files in @{tt}upload_source_dirs@{utt} with an image
extension are uploaded.

@{h2}status

@{tt}status@{utt} is @{tt}pending@{utt} (with @{tt}position@{utt} in the
queue), @{tt}running@{utt}, @{tt}completed@{utt}, @{tt}failed@{utt} (with
@{tt}error@{utt}) or @{tt}unknown@{utt}: neither queued, running nor in the
history -- or, with an @{tt}error@{utt}, the server could not be asked. For
a recorded job the answer adds the workflow and the times:
@{tt}submitted_at@{utt}, @{tt}started_at@{utt}, @{tt}completed_at@{utt},
@{tt}duration_seconds@{utt}.

@{h2}cancel

A queued job is deleted from the queue; a running one is interrupted.
The answer's @{tt}status@{utt}:

@{bullet}@{tt}cancelled@{utt}, with @{tt}was_pending@{utt} or
@{tt}was_running@{utt};
@{bullet}@{tt}already_finished@{utt}, with the job's real
@{tt}job_status@{utt} (and its error), which is recorded;
@{bullet}@{tt}error@{utt}: the server refused, could not be reached, or
does not know the job. An unknown job is never interrupted: an interrupt
stops whatever job the server runs at that moment.

@{h2}server_status, queue, upload_image

These three ask the primary server -- the first in @{tt}servers@{utt}.
@{tt}server_status@{utt} answers @{tt}status: online@{utt} with
@{tt}queue_pending@{utt} and @{tt}queue_running@{utt}, @{tt}offline@{utt}
with an @{tt}error@{utt}, or @{tt}error@{utt} with the HTTP @{tt}code@{utt}
and an @{tt}error@{utt} when the server answers but not with its queue.
@{tt}queue@{utt} answers the
counts, @{tt}pending@{utt} and @{tt}running@{utt}, and the server's whole
queue; an unreachable server is @{tt}status: error@{utt}.
@{tt}upload_image@{utt} reads a file inside @{tt}upload_source_dirs@{utt}
with an image extension and answers @{tt}status: uploaded@{utt} with the
@{tt}filename@{utt} to pass as a workflow parameter. With several servers,
pass the local file to execute instead: the upload then reaches the
server that runs the job.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"operation": "list", "category": "image_generation"}
{"operation": "execute", "workflow_id": "sdxl_txt2img", "parameters": "{\"prompt\": \"a lighthouse at dusk\", \"seed\": 7}"}
{"operation": "wait_for_completion", "prompt_id": "5f0c...", "include_content": true}
@{body}
@endnode

@node results "Waiting, results and files"
@{h1}Waiting, results and files

@{h2}wait_for_completion

Asks the job's server every @{tt}poll_interval@{utt} seconds until the job
is done. The answer's @{tt}status@{utt}:

@{table}
status | When
completed | the job finished; with elapsed_seconds
failed | the job failed on the server, with its error
failed | lost: unknown_threshold_seconds long neither queued, running nor in the history; with unknown_duration
unreachable | the job's server could not be asked for unknown_threshold_seconds; with a hint to ask status later
timeout | not finished after timeout_seconds of the plugin's configuration
cancelled | the agent's run was stopped while it waited
@{body}

The job's outcome is recorded as soon as it is seen -- a lost job as
failed. An unreachable server records nothing: the job may still run, and
stays active until the server answers again. Then a @{tt}status@{utt}
call records it running, completed or failed; a job the server no longer
knows stays active until the panel, or the first tool call after a
restart, marks it lost. With
@{tt}include_content: true@{utt} a completed job is answered as
@{tt}result@{utt} answers it -- always downloaded, and attached -- plus
@{tt}elapsed_seconds@{utt}. The wait has no timeout of its own; the
plugin's @{tt}timeout_seconds@{utt} is the limit.

@{h2}result

Downloads each output file of a finished job into the output folder, named
@{tt}<output_prefix>_<name>@{utt}, and records it. The answer:
@{tt}status: completed@{utt}, @{tt}total_files@{utt} and
@{tt}outputs@{utt} with the lists @{tt}images@{utt}, @{tt}audio@{utt},
@{tt}video@{utt} (animations included) and @{tt}text@{utt}:

@{bullet}a file: @{tt}filename@{utt} and @{tt}subfolder@{utt} on the
server, @{tt}local_path@{utt} (the name in the output folder) and
@{tt}full_path@{utt}, or @{tt}download_error@{utt};
@{bullet}a text: its @{tt}content@{utt}, its @{tt}size_bytes@{utt}, the
@{tt}node_id@{utt} that made it, and the file it was saved to. A text
over 100,000 characters is cut there and marked @{tt}truncated: true@{utt};
the file holds all of it.

With @{tt}download: false@{utt} the files are listed and nothing is saved;
texts still come with their content, and what an earlier call saved stays
recorded for @{tt}load@{utt}. A job that failed answers
@{tt}status: failed@{utt} with ComfyUI's messages. A job not in the
history answers an @{tt}error@{utt} that says whether it is still pending
or running -- then wait --, finished in the meantime -- then ask again --,
failed, or not known at all; a server that cannot be asked is said to be
one, with the reason. An output name from the server that would lead out
of the output folder is not saved; the file gets a
@{tt}download_error@{utt}.

@{h2}load

Attaches files made before, so the model can look at them again; a text
file comes in the answer instead:

@{bullet}@{tt}prompt_id@{utt}: every output @{tt}result@{utt} saved for the
job;
@{bullet}@{tt}file_path@{utt}: one file, relative to the output folder;
@{bullet}@{tt}filename@{utt}: a name looked for in the output folder, then
-- a bare name only -- in its subfolders.

Only files inside the output folder are read; a path that leads out of it
is refused before anything is read. The answer lists
@{tt}loaded_files@{utt} with path, name, type and size, a text with its
@{tt}content@{utt} -- cut at 100,000 characters as in @{tt}result@{utt}. A job whose outputs were never fetched with @{tt}result@{utt}
has nothing to load.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The tool description and every parameter's description come from the
plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; the workflows and their parameters the
model learns from @{tt}list@{utt}, with each parameter's name, type,
whether it is required, its default and description. A refused call
answers @{tt}{"error": "..."}@{utt}, some with a @{tt}hint@{utt}:
"Unknown workflow: x" with the @{tt}available@{utt} ids, "Required
parameter missing: prompt", "parameters is not valid JSON: ...",
"Workflow file not found: ..." with the hint to export it in API format.
When ComfyUI rejects the workflow, its @{tt}node_errors@{utt} come along;
for an image input it cannot find, the hint says to pass the local path
instead.

The chat's status line ends with the result: "Queued 'SDXL
Text-to-Image'", "'SDXL Text-to-Image' completed (41s), 1 output
file(s)", "Job 5f0c...: running", "Job status unavailable: ...".

@{h2}Attached files

With @{tt}include_content@{utt} and with @{tt}load@{utt}, images, sounds
and videos go to the model as attachments of the tool result -- kept apart
from the result's JSON, which only names them. Texts are never attached:
their content is in the JSON, once, up to 100,000 characters each.
Whether the
model can take a kind of file depends on its provider; a file too large
to attach (images and videos over 20 MB, sounds over 25 MB) is left out.
An attached file stays in the conversation and costs input tokens like
the rest of it.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary tool results: appended, nothing earlier
changes. @{tt}queue@{utt} returns the server's whole queue, each entry
with its full workflow -- with many jobs queued, the counts from
@{tt}server_status@{utt} are the cheaper question.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}@{tt}server_status@{utt}, @{tt}queue@{utt} and
@{tt}upload_image@{utt} see only the primary server.
@{bullet}A parameter whose node or field is missing in the workflow file is
skipped with a warning in the log; the job runs with the file's value.
@{bullet}@{tt}output_prefix@{utt} given to @{tt}execute@{utt} is recorded
but not used; the prefix is the one @{tt}result@{utt} or
@{tt}wait_for_completion@{utt} is called with.
@{bullet}Recorded jobs are kept; their files are deleted after
@{tt}cleanup_age_hours@{utt}, and @{tt}load@{utt} finds nothing then.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    comfyui:
      type: comfyui
      enabled: true
      host: "comfy.example"
      port: 8188
      strategy: "least_loaded"
      timeout_seconds: 1200
      servers:
        - host: "comfy.example"
          port: 8188
        - host: "comfy.example"
          port: 8189
      unknown_threshold_seconds: 60
      output_dir: "data/comfyui/outputs"
      workflow_files_dir: "config/comfyui_workflows"
      cleanup_age_hours: 48
      upload_source_dirs:
        - "data/comfyui/outputs"
        - "data/comfyui"
      workflows:
        - id: sdxl_txt2img
          ...
@{body}

An agent sees the tool when its allowlist names it:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+comfyui/*"
@{body}

The plugin also ships an agent, @{tt}comfyui_agent@{utt}, that generates
media with the tool. The plugin needs @{tt}aiohttp@{utt} and a ComfyUI
server the machine can reach.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
host, port | 127.0.0.1, 8188 | the server when servers is not set
servers | -- | a list of host and port; the first is the primary
strategy | least_loaded | least_loaded asks every server for its queue and takes the shortest; random picks one
timeout_seconds | 300 | the longest wait_for_completion waits, and the limit of a request to a server
unknown_threshold_seconds | 60 | how long a job may be unknown, or its server unreachable, before wait_for_completion says so
output_dir | comfyui/outputs in the data directory | where outputs are saved; may contain {session_id}
workflow_files_dir | config/comfyui_workflows | where the workflow files lie
workflows | -- | the workflows agents may run; see Workflows
cleanup_age_hours | 48 | output files older than this are deleted; 0 keeps them
upload_source_dirs | the output folder, comfyui in the data directory and one more asset folder | the only folders upload_image and image inputs read; an empty list turns both off
upload_image_extensions | .png .jpg .jpeg .webp .gif .bmp | the files upload_image takes
@{body}

With least_loaded, a server that did not answer is skipped for a minute;
when none answers, the primary gets the job. With
@{tt}{session_id}@{utt} in @{tt}output_dir@{utt} each session saves into a
folder of its own. Old files are deleted on the first tool call and after
each @{tt}result@{utt}.

The jobs are recorded in @{tt}jobs.db@{utt}, next to the output folder
(@{tt}data/comfyui/jobs.db@{utt} by default). Instances of the plugin with
the same output folder share it; each leaves the jobs of the other's
servers alone.
@endnode

@node workflows "Workflows"
@{h1}Workflows

In ComfyUI, build the workflow and export it with @{b}Save (API
Format)@{ub} into @{tt}workflow_files_dir@{utt}. Then name it in the
plugin's configuration with the inputs an agent may set:

@{code yaml}
workflows:
  - id: sdxl_txt2img
    name: "SDXL Text-to-Image"
    description: "An image from a text prompt"
    category: image_generation
    workflow_file: sdxl_txt2img.json
    parameters:
      - {name: prompt, type: string, required: true, node_id: "6", field: inputs.text,
         description: "What the image shows"}
      - {name: seed, type: integer, default: 42, node_id: "3", field: inputs.seed}
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Meaning
id | what execute names
name, description, category | shown by list; category general when left out
workflow_file | the file in workflow_files_dir; without it <id>.json
parameters | the inputs an agent may set
@{body}

A parameter's @{tt}node_id@{utt} is the number ComfyUI shows on the node
(@{tt}#6@{utt}), @{tt}field@{utt} the dot path of the value in the node
(@{tt}inputs.text@{utt}). The value is written as the agent sends it;
@{tt}type@{utt} only tells the model what to send. The workflow file is
read on every execute: a changed file needs no restart.
@endnode
